# THE RIGHT TO A FIRST CHANCE

## Doors for those already inside

Humanity has a peculiar talent for building perfectly reasonable rules that, once assembled, produce a very strange world. A fund wants to support professional artists, so it asks applicants to prove professionalism; an employer wants to reduce hiring risk, so an entry-level job asks for experience; a research fund wants projects that can be completed, so it looks at previous grants and publications; a bank wants its money back, so it prefers people with a credit history; a landlord wants a reliable tenant, so it asks for stable income and rental history; a state wants to protect social programmes from error and fraud, so it asks a person to prove that assistance is genuinely needed. Each rule can make sense on its own. Step back a few metres, however, and a systemic paradox appears: to receive help **entering** a system, you increasingly have to show evidence that you somehow **entered it already without the help**.

The door exists, and above it there may even be a bright sign saying ENTRY POINT. The handle is on the inside. Naturally, there is a PDF explaining the procedure.

## An art competition. Did you bring your structural mechanics certificate?

Imagine an art competition. A person arrives to show the work, but before anyone looks at the portfolio they are asked to pass an exam in strength of materials, collect thirteen certificates, obtain two recommendations from academics, and bring confirmation from an accountant, two miners and a Hawaiian surfer that they are, in fact, an artist. There is also a form. No, there is no pen. Everything is modern now: the form is digital. First create an account, verify the email, install the app, confirm the app through the website, enable two-factor authentication and upload a document the system already received last year. The response will arrive only inside the app. Why not by email? Because the institution cares about personal data. Why, then, does the app ask for a name, address, phone number, date of birth, passport photograph, notification permission and show a lively interest in location? For security.

Forty minutes later, the person who came to an art competition has successfully demonstrated the ability to survive electronic identification, navigate institutional portals, maintain stable internet access, tolerate frustration and, in our deliberately excessive example, locate a friendly academic. One small question remains: **did anyone look at the paintings?**

The joke works because the mechanism is not entirely fictional. An administrative procedure stops being a neutral wrapper when access to artistic evaluation primarily tests digital literacy, bureaucratic competence, social connections, the ability to read regulatory language and the capacity to endure friction. The system then partly selects not the strongest artists, but the strongest artists **among those who survived the secondary examination**. This does not make every such procedure unfair. It does mean that the procedure changes the sample and should therefore be visible as part of the methodology rather than treated as administrative weather.

## When an artist is already artist enough

Sociological evidence shows that this effect is not limited to jokes about forms. Julia Peters and Henk Roose examined public funding of visual artists in Flanders from 1965 to 2015 and found a pronounced **Matthew effect**: artists with stronger exhibition histories and prior grant success had better chances of receiving subsequent public support. Particularly revealing is the historical shift: before the 1990s, first-time applicants were more often given an opportunity to enter the grant system, while a later policy emphasis on professionalisation made the first grant harder to obtain. The study does not argue that established professionals should not be supported. It shows something subtler: a policy that becomes very good at supporting an already professional field may become less effective at **producing new participants in that field**. [Peters & Roose, *Poetics*, 2022](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.poetic.2021.101578).

Two functions are easily hidden inside the single word support. There is support for the development of an already established participant, where implementation history, team capacity, reputation and institutional competence may be entirely appropriate criteria. Then there is support for entry, which exists precisely at the moment when some of those proofs **cannot yet exist**, because the first opportunity is what would create them. When the same selection logic tries to solve both problems, development of the new can quietly become a test of how closely the new already resembles the old professional field.

A professional artist does not hatch from an egg holding a CV, three completed grants, an institutional partner and a cash-flow spreadsheet in which even the studio kettle has a KPI. If a cultural system wants not only to service a field but to preserve its ability to generate new participants, perhaps it needs a separate architecture for first entry, one in which the work can be seen before the system asks how many other institutions have already agreed to see it.

## Junior. Entry level. Three years of experience

In the labour market, the same mechanism has become a meme. A graduate finds a vacancy marked Junior or Entry level and opens it to discover that two or three years of relevant professional experience are required. For the first job, please provide evidence of the previous first job; candidates supply their own time machine. The International Labour Organization has explicitly described the Catch-22: young people cannot get work without experience and cannot acquire experience because they cannot get the first job. Internships and apprenticeships make sense as bridges between education and employment when they actually train people rather than disguising unpaid labour. [ILO, *Internships: Head start or labour trap?*](https://www.ilo.org/resource/article/internships-head-start-or-labour-trap).

Later ILO research adds an important detail: paid and well-designed internships are associated with better post-internship labour outcomes, particularly when there is mentoring, working conditions resemble ordinary employment and the placement lasts long enough for real competencies to develop. [O’Higgins & Pinedo, ILO, 2018](https://www.ilo.org/publications/interns-and-outcomes-just-how-effective-are-internships-bridge-stable). Social inequality enters almost without announcing itself. An unpaid opportunity may formally be open to everyone, but it is easier to take if housing, family support, savings or some other reserve of time already exists. We may believe we are selecting for motivation while partly selecting for the ability to **afford a period of demonstrating motivation without pay**.

This is not merely a minor unfairness at the starting line. Early unemployment and unstable entry can leave long effects on income, employment and later trajectories, so the first entry point can influence not only the first salary but how later institutions read the person’s biography. One rejection does not define a life, but enough locally reasonable rejections can create a history that a future employer reads as evidence of a weak trajectory. The system digs a hole, the person falls, and years later the limp risks being interpreted as evidence that the person was never good at walking.

## Success enjoys meeting success

Robert K. Merton described the **Matthew effect** in science in 1968: already recognised scientists can receive more recognition and visibility for contributions, while less recognised researchers remain less visible. The idea became part of the broader account of cumulative advantage, where a small initial difference can generate a much larger later difference. [Merton, *Science*, 1968](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.159.3810.56).

The mechanism is especially visible in research funding. Thijs Bol, Mathijs de Vaan and Arnout van de Rijt compared early-career researchers immediately above and below a funding threshold. Applicants were close in evaluation, but those who narrowly obtained early funding accumulated substantially more subsequent funding. The authors describe two mutually reinforcing processes: previous winners are evaluated more positively in later competitions, and successful applicants are themselves more likely to apply again. [Bol, de Vaan & van de Rijt, *PNAS*, 2018](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1719557115).

The point can be stated without turning it into an equation. **A person’s merit is not the same thing as previous access to a system, but previous access can help merit become visible, tested and cumulative.** A first grant enables a project, the project enables a publication, the publication creates contacts, the contacts create invitations, and the invitations create further evidence on a CV. Ten years later, the difference between two people may be enormous even though it began as a small difference partly shaped by one early institutional decision. Past achievement remains useful evidence, but we should not quietly record the entire infrastructure of prior opportunity as a pure personal property of the winner.

This is not an attack on the idea that competence, work and results deserve recognition. It is a request to account more precisely for what we call merit. Talent with five attempts and comparable talent with one attempt are not participating in the same experiment. A person who swims across a river with their hands tied may be extraordinary; that is still a poor argument for building bridges that tie everyone’s hands first.

## The tragedy of reasonable rules

The deeper ethical turn begins when we refuse the comfort of a villain. It would be easy to explain everything through a malicious bureaucrat, a greedy fund, a lazy recruiter or a heartless bank. In reality, every actor may be behaving rationally within a local frame: the fund reduces the risk of project failure, the employer reduces the risk of a bad hire, the bank reduces default risk, the landlord reduces non-payment risk, and the state reduces error and fraud. No participant needs to be foolish or cruel for a cruel architecture to emerge in the aggregate.

When every institution minimises **its own** risk, uncertainty does not disappear. It changes address. Very often the uncertainty of the first step moves to the person standing at the entrance. The young artist finances the years before the first professional opportunity; the young worker finances the acquisition of “experience”; the founder is expected to prove demand before the capital that was supposed to help test demand arrives; the migrant must decode an administrative system in a language not yet mastered; the person without money must find time, energy and documents to obtain assistance because of the absence of money.

The tragedy is systemic: locally rational institutions can collectively privatise safety and transfer uncertainty to newcomers. The least protected person may receive the largest share of risk precisely because there is not yet enough history to prove reliability. The useful question is therefore not only who is at fault, but **whether the risk of the first attempt has been distributed fairly between the individual and the system**.

## Assistance that must first be survived

